<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Dilip's Log: webdev</title><subtitle>Dilip's Log</subtitle><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://idlip.in/" /><link rel="self" type="application/atom+xml" href="https://idlip.in/tags/webdev/rss.xml" /><id>https://idlip.in/tags/webdev/rss.xml</id><updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00Z</updated><author><name>Dilip | Zororg</name></author><generator>Denden</generator><rights>Copyright © 2023-2026, Dilip | Zororg; All rights reserved.</rights><entry><title>Remaking the site: Hakimacs to Kenbun</title><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://idlip.in/log/remake-kenbun/" /><id>https://idlip.in/log/remake-kenbun/</id><published>2026-11-19T00:00:00Z</published><updated>2026-11-20T00:00:00Z</updated><category term="denden" label="denden" scheme="https://idlip.in/tags/"></category><category term="webdev" label="webdev" scheme="https://idlip.in/tags/"></category><summary>In January I wrote about Revamp the blogs and in march about Replenish theme - its Emacsy. Now, here we go once more. Same same, but really different

The old look, Hakimacs, painted every page as an Emacs window: a menu bar on top, a modeline at the bottom, an echo area, dropdowns, frosted glass. I loved how it looked. But over time I kept adding &quot;helpers for reading&quot; and now the helpers look noisier than the reading. I like how Bear Blog, Miniflux Lobsters just let you read, with no BS. So this time the rule was simple: the page exists to be read, and the CSS exists to be maintained.</summary><content type="html">&lt;p&gt;
In January I wrote about &lt;a href=&quot;/log/revamp-blog-2026/&quot;&gt;Revamp the blogs&lt;/a&gt; and in march about &lt;a href=&quot;/log/emacs-theme-site/&quot;&gt;Replenish theme - its Emacsy&lt;/a&gt;. Now, here we go once more. &lt;i&gt;Same same, but really different&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The old look, &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/idlip/hakimacs&quot;&gt;Hakimacs&lt;/a&gt;, painted every page as an Emacs window: a menu bar on top, a modeline at the bottom, an echo area, dropdowns, frosted glass. I loved how it looked. But over time I kept adding &quot;helpers for reading&quot; and now the helpers look noisier than the reading. I like how &lt;a href=&quot;https://bearblog.dev/&quot;&gt;Bear Blog&lt;/a&gt;, Miniflux &lt;a href=&quot;https://lobste.rs/&quot;&gt;Lobsters&lt;/a&gt; just let you read, with no BS. So this time the rule was simple: the page exists to be read, and the CSS exists to be maintained.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The new look is &lt;i&gt;Kenbun&lt;/i&gt;, named after &lt;a href=&quot;https://onepiece.fandom.com/wiki/Haki/Observation_Haki&quot;&gt;Kenbunshoku Haki&lt;/a&gt;, the observation Haki (its all One Piece-d). Sense what matters, drop the noise.
&lt;/p&gt;
