&lt;div id=&quot;outline-container-what-i-kept-in-mind&quot; class=&quot;outline-2&quot;&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;what-i-kept-in-mind&quot;&gt;What I kept in mind&lt;a class=&quot;heading-anchor&quot; href=&quot;#what-i-kept-in-mind&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to this section&quot;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;outline-text-2&quot; id=&quot;text-what-i-kept-in-mind&quot;&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;org-ul&quot;&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Style plain HTML elements first, and keep a short list of shared pieces. A new page reuses them; it
never gets its own CSS.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Trust the browser: &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;details&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; for folding, &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;dialog&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; for the palette, a popover for small notes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Readable or gone. Every colour scheme is checked for contrast when the site is built, and only 140 pass AAA out of 306 schemes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
&lt;i&gt;Read more, write more, give more&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
